        </div>
        <figure>
          <img src="/article-placeholder/wall-street-paul-strand-1915.jpg" width="3132" height="2465" alt="Pedestrians and their long shadows pass the deep windows of a Wall Street building." fetchpriority="high" />
          <figcaption>Detail from <a href={imageSource}><cite>Wall Street</cite>, Paul Strand</a>, 1915.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </header>
      <article aria-label="Article text">
        <p>What changes when we look at something for a little longer? A photograph first appears to be a single thing: a street, a building, a handful of people on their way somewhere. Then the picture begins to separate into smaller observations:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>The shadows move in a different direction from the people.</li>
          <li>The windows seem larger than the building that contains them.</li>
          <li>An ordinary morning has become something we can return to.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>The scene has not changed. What changes is the amount of it we are able to notice.</p>
        <h2>Ordinary things remain</h2>
        <p>A familiar place can become almost invisible. We learn its route, its entrances, the time it takes to cross it. These are useful ways of knowing a place, but they leave much of it untouched. There is still the light on a wall, the sound of a door closing, the small variations that make one day different from another.</p>
        <p>Looking does not have to become a project. There need not be a conclusion waiting at the end of it. Sometimes a photograph is useful simply because it gives attention somewhere to rest.</p>
        <h2>A little more time</h2>
        <p>In <a href={imageSource}>Strand’s photograph</a>, the people continue across the pavement. We do not know where they are going. Their shadows stretch behind them, briefly joining the geometry of the building. The picture holds these different scales together without explaining them.</p>
        <p>Perhaps that is enough for now: an image, a few sentences, and the possibility of coming back to them.</p>
